{"id":"gen-1790978040-Zt7DWvw3TonoT9p6K38x","object":"chat.completion","created":1790978040,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6112-raw-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 3,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 3,\n        \"d\": 1,\n        \"e\": 1,\n        \"f\": 0,\n        \"g\": 3,\n        \"h\": 0,\n        \"i\": 2,\n        \"j\": 3,\n        \"k\": 1,\n        \"l\": 3,\n        \"m\": 1,\n        \"n\": 0,\n        \"o\": 0,\n        \"p\": 1\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6112\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6112\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve a CSP: variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p each domain {0,1,2,3}. Constraints c1-c16 are binary constraints on pairs, each specifying allowed pairs (ordered). Also there are \"bridge\" constraints c17-c20 linking variables across components: c17: allowed pairs for (b,e) are only [0,1] and [0,0] (so b=0, e=0 or b=0, e=1). c18: (f,i) allowed pairs: [0,2] and [3,3]. c19: (j,m) allowed: [3,1] and [3,2]. c20: (n,a) allowed: [0,3] and [3,0].\n\nWe need to find a complete assignment that satisfies all constraints. Also local requirement: \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" Not entirely clear: The answer_requirements say: \"Give one complete assignment for a through p satisfying all twenty constraints.\" So we need to produce a single assignment. \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" That seems contradictory; maybe we need to output all satisfying tuples for each component? But the envelope expects assignment only. Might be extraneous description. The main requirement: Return a complete assignment for all 16 variables satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe need to solve the CSP. Let's attempt systematically.\n\nWe have constraints c1-c16: each constraint is a binary allowed pairs list for a specific ordered pair of variables. We need to assign values to a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p ∈ {0,1,2,3} such that each pair (var1,var2) belongs to allowed_pairs for that constraint.\n\nThe constraints form a cycle: a-b (c1), b-c (c2), c-d (c3), d-a (c4), e-f (c5), f-g (c6), g-h (c7), h-e (c8), i-j (c9), j-k (c10), k-l (c11), l-i (c12), m-n (c13), n-o (c14), o-p (c15), p-m (c16). Bridges: b-e (c17), f-i (c18), j-m (c19), n-a (c20). So we have two disconnected components? Actually there are three groups: group1 includes a,b,c,d and also e,f,g,h via bridges, plus i,j,k,l via bridges? Let's see connections: Bridges connect across groups: b-e links group1 (a,b,c,d) to group2 (e,f,g,h). f-i links group2 to group3 (i,j,k,l). j-m links group3 to group4 (m,n,o,p). n-a links group4 back to group1. So overall it's a single connected graph.\n\nWe need to satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe can brute force search manually or via reasoning.\n\nWe have small domain size 4, 16 variables, total combos 4^16 huge. But constraints heavily restrict.\n\nLet's enumerate constraints.\n\nc1 (a,b): allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (2,2), (2,3), (0,3), (1,3). So allowed (a,b):\n- a=3,b=0\n- a=0,b=2\n- a=2,b=2\n- a=2,b=3\n- a=0,b=3\n- a=1,b=3\n\nc2 (b,c): allowed: (0,3), (2,3), (1,2), (3,2), (2,1), (3,0). So (b,c):\n- b=0,c=3\n- b=2,c=3\n- b=1,c=2\n- b=3,c=2\n- b=2,c=1\n- b=3,c=0\n\nc3 (c,d): allowed: (3,1), (0,3), (3,3), (3,0), (0,2), (2,2). So (c,d):\n- c=3,d=1\n- c=0,d=3\n- c=3,d=3\n- c=3,d=0\n- c=0,d=2\n- c=2,d=2\n\nc4 (d,a): allowed: (1,3), (1,0), (1,1), (0,1), (3,2), (2,1). So (d,a):\n- d=1,a=3\n- d=1,a=0\n- d=1,a=1\n- d=0,a=1\n- d=3,a=2\n- d=2,a=1\n\nc5 (e,f): allowed: (1,0), (1,3), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2), (0,1). So (e,f):\n- e=1,f=0\n- e=1,f=3\n- e=2,f=2\n- e=2,f=0\n- e=3,f=2\n- e=0,f=1\n\nc6 (f,g): allowed: (0,3), (2,0), (3,3), (2,2), (1,3), (3,1). So (f,g):\n- f=0,g=3\n- f=2,g=0\n- f=3,g=3\n- f=2,g=2\n- f=1,g=3\n- f=3,g=1\n\nc7 (g,h): allowed: (3,0), (1,0), (2,1), (2,3), (1,3), (2,0). So (g,h):\n- g=3,h=0\n- g=1,h=0\n- g=2,h=1\n- g=2,h=3\n- g=1,h=3\n- g=2,h=0\n\nc8 (h,e): allowed: (0,1), (0,0), (1,2), (3,3), (3,1), (1,0). So (h,e):\n- h=0,e=1\n- h=0,e=0\n- h=1,e=2\n- h=3,e=3\n- h=3,e=1\n- h=1,e=0\n\nc9 (i,j): allowed: (2,3), (0,0), (3,0), (0,2), (2,1), (0,3). So (i,j):\n- i=2,j=3\n- i=0,j=0\n- i=3,j=0\n- i=0,j=2\n- i=2,j=1\n- i=0,j=3\n\nc10 (j,k): allowed: (3,1), (3,3), (1,3), (2,3), (1,2), (2,1). So (j,k):\n- j=3,k=1\n- j=3,k=3\n- j=1,k=3\n- j=2,k=3\n- j=1,k=2\n- j=2,k=1\n\nc11 (k,l): allowed: (1,3), (1,1), (3,2), (0,3), (3,0), (0,2). So (k,l):\n- k=1,l=3\n- k=1,l=1\n- k=3,l=2\n- k=0,l=3\n- k=3,l=0\n- k=0,l=2\n\nc12 (l,i): allowed: (3,2), (0,1), (2,0), (1,0), (1,3), (3,0). So (l,i):\n- l=3,i=2\n- l=0,i=1\n- l=2,i=0\n- l=1,i=0\n- l=1,i=3\n- l=3,i=0\n\nc13 (m,n): allowed: (1,0), (2,3), (1,3), (0,2), (1,1), (3,0). So (m,n):\n- m=1,n=0\n- m=2,n=3\n- m=1,n=3\n- m=0,n=2\n- m=1,n=1\n- m=3,n=0\n\nc14 (n,o): allowed: (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,3). So (n,o):\n- n=0,o=0\n- n=1,o=3\n- n=0,o=2\n- n=2,o=1\n- n=2,o=3\n- n=3,o=3\n\nc15 (o,p): allowed: (0,1), (0,0), (1,3), (1,0), (2,3), (3,2). So (o,p):\n- o=0,p=1\n- o=0,p=0\n- o=1,p=3\n- o=1,p=0\n- o=2,p=3\n- o=3,p=2\n\nc16 (p,m): allowed: (1,1), (3,3), (0,2), (1,0), (2,3), (3,0). So (p,m):\n- p=1,m=1\n- p=3,m=3\n- p=0,m=2\n- p=1,m=0\n- p=2,m=3\n- p=3,m=0\n\nc17 (b,e): allowed: (0,1), (0,0). So b=0,e=0 or b=0,e=1.\n\nc18 (f,i): allowed: (0,2), (3,3). So f=0,i=2 OR f=3,i=3.\n\nc19 (j,m): allowed: (3,1), (3,2). So j=3,m=1 OR j=3,m=2.\n\nc20 (n,a): allowed: (0,3), (3,0). So n=0,a=3 OR n=3,a=0.\n\nWe need to assign all variables accordingly.\n\nLet's approach systematically.\n\nWe have many constraints. Let's try to reduce possibilities.\n\nFirst, c17 forces b=0. So b=0. Then c17's allowed pairs: (b,e) = (0,1) or (0,0). So e ∈ {0,1}.\n\nThus b=0.\n\nNow c1 (a,b): b=0. Allowed pairs for (a,b):\n- (3,0) => a=3,b=0 allowed.\n- (0,2) => a=0,b=2 not allowed because b=0.\n- (2,2) => b=2 not allowed.\n- (2,3) => b=3 not allowed.\n- (0,3) => b=3 not allowed.\n- (1,3) => b=3 not allowed.\n\nThus only allowed pair with b=0 is (3,0). So a=3.\n\nThus a=3.\n\nNow c4 (d,a): a=3. Allowed pairs for (d,a):\n- (1,3): d=1,a=3 allowed.\n- (1,0): a=0 not allowed.\n- (1,1): a=1 not allowed.\n- (0,1): a=1 not allowed.\n- (3,2): a=2 not allowed.\n- (2,1): a=1 not allowed.\n\nThus only allowed is (d=1,a=3). So d=1.\n\nThus d=1.\n\nNow c3 (c,d): d=1. Allowed pairs for (c,d):\n- (3,1): c=3,d=1 allowed.\n- (0,3): d=3 not allowed.\n- (3,3): d=3 not allowed.\n- (3,0): d=0 not allowed.\n- (0,2): d=2 not allowed.\n- (2,2): d=2 not allowed.\n\nThus only allowed is (c=3,d=1). So c=3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c2 (b,c): b=0,c=3. Check allowed pairs for (b,c):\n- (0,3): b=0,c=3 allowed.\n- (2,3): b=2 not allowed.\n- (1,2): b=1 not allowed.\n- (3,2): b=3 not allowed.\n- (2,1): b=2 not allowed.\n- (3,0): b=3 not allowed.\n\nThus only allowed is (0,3). So this matches. Good.\n\nThus a,b,c,d solved: a=3,b=0,c=3,d=1.\n\nNow we have e from c17: b=0 => e ∈ {0,1}. So e=0 or 1.\n\nNow c5 (e,f): depends on e. Let's list possibilities for e.\n\nCase 1: e=0. Then c5 allowed pairs (e,f):\n- (1,0): e=1 not allowed.\n- (1,3): e=1 not allowed.\n- (2,2): e=2 not allowed.\n- (2,0): e=2 not allowed.\n- (3,2): e=3 not allowed.\n- (0,1): e=0,f=1 allowed.\n\nThus if e=0, then f must be 1.\n\nCase 2: e=1. Then c5 allowed pairs:\n- (1,0): e=1,f=0 allowed.\n- (1,3): e=1,f=3 allowed.\n- (2,2): e=2 not allowed.\n- (2,0): e=2 not allowed.\n- (3,2): e=3 not allowed.\n- (0,1): e=0 not allowed.\n\nThus if e=1, f ∈ {0,3}.\n\nThus possible (e,f) combos:\n- (0,1)\n- (1,0)\n- (1,3)\n\nNow c6 (f,g): depends on f. Let's consider each f.\n\nIf f=1: c6 allowed (f,g):\n- (0,3): f=0 not allowed.\n- (2,0): f=2 not allowed.\n- (3,3): f=3 not allowed.\n- (2,2): f=2 not allowed.\n- (1,3): f=1,g=3 allowed.\n- (3,1): f=3 not allowed.\n\nThus if f=1, then g=3.\n\nIf f=0: c6 allowed:\n- (0,3): f=0,g=3 allowed.\n- (2,0): f=2 not allowed.\n- (3,3): f=3 not allowed.\n- (2,2): f=2 not allowed.\n- (1,3): f=1 not allowed.\n- (3,1): f=3 not allowed.\n\nThus if f=0, then g=3.\n\nIf f=3: c6 allowed:\n- (0,3): f=0 not allowed.\n- (2,0): f=2 not allowed.\n- (3,3): f=3,g=3 allowed.\n- (2,2): f=2 not allowed.\n- (1,3): f=1 not allowed.\n- (3,1): f=3,g=1 allowed.\n\nThus if f=3, then g ∈ {3,1}.\n\nThus g possibilities:\n- If f=1 => g=3.\n- If f=0 => g=3.\n- If f=3 => g=3 or g=1.\n\nNow c7 (g,h): allowed pairs for (g,h):\n- (3,0): g=3,h=0 allowed.\n- (1,0): g=1,h=0 allowed.\n- (2,1): g=2,h=1 not applicable (g not 2).\n- (2,3): g=2,h=3 not applicable.\n- (1,3): g=1,h=3 allowed.\n- (2,0): g=2,h=0 not applicable.\n\nThus g can be 3 or 1. If g=3, h can be 0 (via (3,0)) or maybe also? Check (g=3) other allowed pairs: (3,0) only? There's also (3,?) Not other. So g=3 => h=0.\n\nIf g=1, then h can be 0 (via (1,0)) or 3 (via (1,3)). So h ∈ {0,3} if g=1.\n\nNow c8 (h,e): allowed pairs for (h,e):\n- (0,1): h=0,e=1 allowed.\n- (0,0): h=0,e=0 allowed.\n- (1,2): h=1,e=2 not applicable (h not 1).\n- (3,3): h=3,e=3 not applicable (e not 3 maybe).\n- (3,1): h=3,e=1 allowed.\n- (1,0): h=1,e=0 not applicable.\n\nThus possibilities:\n- If h=0: e can be 1 (via (0,1)) or 0 (via (0,0)). So e ∈ {0,1} when h=0.\n- If h=3: e can be 1 (via (3,1)) or 3 (via (3,3)). So e ∈ {1,3} when h=3.\n- If h=1: not allowed (no pair with h=1? Actually (1,2) and (1,0) require e=2 or e=0; but (1,2) is allowed but e=2 not in domain? e domain is 0-3, e=2 possible. However (1,2) is allowed: h=1,e=2. Also (1,0) allowed: h=1,e=0. So h=1 also possible: e ∈ {2,0}. But is h=1 reachable? g=2? But g cannot be 2 based on earlier constraints? Let's check: c7 allowed g values: 3,1,2? Actually c7 allowed pairs include (2,1) and (2,3) and (2,0) but those require g=2. But we have g from earlier: g can be 3 or 1 (if f=3 maybe also 1). Actually from c6, g can be 3 or 1 (if f=3). So g cannot be 2. So h cannot be 1 because g cannot be 2. So h ∈ {0,3}.\n\nThus h must be 0 or 3.\n\nNow combine with earlier constraints: h is determined by g.\n\nCase A: g=3 => h=0 (only). Then h=0. Then c8: h=0 => e ∈ {0,1}.\n\nCase B: g=1 => h ∈ {0,3}. So possibilities: (g=1,h=0) or (g=1,h=3).\n\nNow let's examine the possibilities for e and f and g consistent with c5-c8.\n\nWe have initial possibilities for (e,f):\n- (0,1)\n- (1,0)\n- (1,3)\n\nNow evaluate each:\n\nCase (e=0,f=1):\n- f=1 => from c6, g=3.\n- Then g=3 => h=0.\n- Then h=0 => c8 allows e=0 or 1. e=0 matches, ok.\nThus (e=0,f=1,g=3,h=0) is consistent.\n\nCheck c8: (h=0,e=0) is allowed (0,0). Good.\n\nThus one consistent branch.\n\nCase (e=1,f=0):\n- f=0 => c6: g=3.\n- g=3 => h=0.\n- h=0 => c8 allows e=0 or 1. e=1 allowed (0,1) pair.\nThus (e=1,f=0,g=3,h=0) consistent.\n\nCase (e=1,f=3):\n- f=3 => c6: g ∈ {3,1}.\nWe need to consider subcases:\n\nSubcase (e=1,f=3,g=3):\n- g=3 => h=0.\n- h=0 => c8 allows e=0 or 1. e=1 allowed.\nThus (e=1,f=3,g=3,h=0) consistent.\n\nSubcase (e=1,f=3,g=1):\n- g=1 => h ∈ {0,3}.\nNow need to satisfy c8 with h and e=1.\n\nIf h=0: c8 allows e=0 or 1 => e=1 ok.\nIf h=3: c8 allows e=1 or 3 => e=1 ok.\nThus both h=0 and h=3 possible.\n\nThus (e=1,f=3,g=1,h=0) and (e=1,f=3,g=1,h=3) are possible.\n\nThus many possibilities for e,f,g,h.\n\nNow we also have constraints linking to other variables via bridges: c17 already used (b,e) satisfied: b=0, e ∈ {0,1} consistent. c18 (f,i): allowed pairs: (0,2) or (3,3). So depending on f, i is determined.\n\nc19 (j,m): j=3,m ∈ {1,2}.\n\nc20 (n,a): a=3 => n must be? Allowed pairs: (0,3) => n=0,a=3 allowed; (3,0) => n=3,a=0 not allowed because a=3. So only n=0 works. So n=0.\n\nThus n=0.\n\nNow c13 (m,n): n=0. Allowed pairs for (m,n):\n- (1,0): m=1,n=0 allowed.\n- (2,3): n=3 not allowed.\n- (1,3): n=3 not allowed.\n- (0,2): n=2 not allowed.\n- (1,1): n=1 not allowed.\n- (3,0): m=3,n=0 allowed.\n\nThus m ∈ {1,3}.\n\nNow c14 (n,o): n=0. Allowed pairs for (n,o):\n- (0,0): o=0 allowed.\n- (1,3): n=1 not allowed.\n- (0,2): o=2 allowed.\n- (2,1): n=2 not allowed.\n- (2,3): n=2 not allowed.\n- (3,3): n=3 not allowed.\n\nThus o ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow c15 (o,p): depends on o.\n\nIf o=0: allowed pairs:\n- (0,1): p=1 allowed.\n- (0,0): p=0 allowed.\n- (1,3): o=1 not allowed.\n- (1,0): o=1 not allowed.\n- (2,3): o=2 not allowed.\n- (3,2): o=3 not allowed.\n\nThus if o=0, p ∈ {0,1}.\n\nIf o=2: allowed pairs:\n- (0,1): o=0 not allowed.\n- (0,0): o=0 not allowed.\n- (1,3): o=1 not allowed.\n- (1,0): o=1 not allowed.\n- (2,3): p=3 allowed.\n- (3,2): o=3 not allowed.\n\nThus if o=2, p=3.\n\nThus p possibilities:\n- If o=0 => p ∈ {0,1}\n- If o=2 => p=3\n\nNow c16 (p,m): allowed pairs for (p,m):\n- (1,1): p=1,m=1 allowed.\n- (3,3): p=3,m=3 allowed.\n- (0,2): p=0,m=2 allowed.\n- (1,0): p=1,m=0 allowed.\n- (2,3): p=2,m=3 allowed.\n- (3,0): p=3,m=0 allowed.\n\nThus given p and m must satisfy one of these.\n\nWe have m ∈ {1,3} from earlier. So possible combos:\n\nIf m=1:\n- Allowed pairs with m=1:\n  - (1,1): p=1\n  - (1,0): p=1,m=0 not relevant.\n  - (3,3): p=3,m=3 not relevant.\n  - (0,2): m=2 not relevant.\n  - (2,3): m=3 not relevant.\n  - (3,0): m=0 not relevant.\n\nThus only p=1 works for m=1. So if m=1, then p must be 1.\n\nIf m=3:\n- Allowed pairs with m=3:\n  - (3,3): p=3\n  - (2,3): p=2\n  - (3,0): p=3,m=0 not relevant.\n  - (1,1): m=1 not relevant.\n  - (1,0): m=0 not relevant.\n  - (0,2): m=2 not relevant.\n\nThus p ∈ {2,3} for m=3.\n\nNow combine with earlier constraints on p from o.\n\nIf o=0 => p ∈ {0,1}. Intersection with p possibilities:\n\n- If m=1 => p must be 1. That fits o=0 (p=1 allowed). So (o=0,p=1,m=1) works.\n- If m=3 => p ∈ {2,3}. Intersection with {0,1} is empty. So if o=0, m cannot be 3. So m must be 1.\n\nThus we deduce: o=0, m=1, p=1.\n\nNow check if o=2 possible? If o=2 => p=3. Then p=3 must satisfy c16 with m.\n\n- If m=1 => p=3 not allowed (p must be 1). So m cannot be 1.\n- If m=3 => p=3 allowed (c16 (3,3)). So m=3, p=3, o=2 works.\n\nThus second possible branch: o=2, p=3, m=3.\n\nThus we have two possible branches for (m,n,o,p):\nBranch A: n=0, m=1, o=0, p=1.\nBranch B: n=0, m=3, o=2, p=3.\n\nNow we need to satisfy other constraints: c13 (m,n) satisfied by both branches (m=1,n=0) is allowed (1,0). (m=3,n=0) also allowed (3,0). Good.\n\nc14 (n,o) satisfied: n=0,o=0 allowed (0,0). n=0,o=2 allowed (0,2). Good.\n\nc15 (o,p) satisfied: o=0,p=1 allowed (0,1). o=2,p=3 allowed (2,3). Good.\n\nc16 (p,m) satisfied: p=1,m=1 allowed (1,1). p=3,m=3 allowed (3,3). Good.\n\nThus both branches are viable.\n\nNow we need to handle other variables: i,j,k,l from the other component.\n\nWe have constraints:\n\nc9 (i,j): allowed pairs:\n- (2,3): i=2,j=3\n- (0,0): i=0,j=0\n- (3,0): i=3,j=0\n- (0,2): i=0,j=2\n- (2,1): i=2,j=1\n- (0,3): i=0,j=3\n\nThus i can be 0,2,3. j accordingly.\n\nc10 (j,k): allowed pairs:\n- (3,1): j=3,k=1\n- (3,3): j=3,k=3\n- (1,3): j=1,k=3\n- (2,3): j=2,k=3\n- (1,2): j=1,k=2\n- (2,1): j=2,k=1\n\nThus j can be 1,2,3. k accordingly.\n\nc11 (k,l): allowed pairs:\n- (1,3): k=1,l=3\n- (1,1): k=1,l=1\n- (3,2): k=3,l=2\n- (0,3): k=0,l=3\n- (3,0): k=3,l=0\n- (0,2): k=0,l=2\n\nThus k can be 0,1,3. l accordingly.\n\nc12 (l,i): allowed pairs:\n- (3,2): l=3,i=2\n- (0,1): l=0,i=1\n- (2,0): l=2,i=0\n- (1,0): l=1,i=0\n- (1,3): l=1,i=3\n- (3,0): l=3,i=0\n\nThus l can be 0,1,2,3. i accordingly.\n\nc18 (f,i): allowed pairs: (0,2) or (3,3). So depending on f.\n\nc19 (j,m): allowed pairs: (3,1) or (3,2). So j must be 3, and m is 1 or 2 accordingly. Since we have m already determined in branch A (m=1) or branch B (m=3). Let's check c19: allowed (j,m) pairs: (3,1) and (3,2). So j must be 3, and m must be either 1 or 2. So m must be 1 or 2. In branch A, m=1 satisfies. In branch B, m=3 does NOT satisfy c19 because m=3 not allowed. So branch B is invalid because c19 would be violated (j=3,m=3 not allowed). Thus we must choose branch A: m=1, n=0, o=0, p=1.\n\nThus we have m=1, p=1.\n\nThus we have fixed m,n,o,p.\n\nNow c19: j=3, m=1. So j=3.\n\nThus j=3.\n\nNow c10 (j,k): j=3. Allowed pairs for (j,k):\n- (3,1): k=1\n- (3,3): k=3\n\nThus k ∈ {1,3}.\n\nNow c11 (k,l): depends on k.\n\nIf k=1: allowed (k,l):\n- (1,3): l=3\n- (1,1): l=1\n\nThus l ∈ {1,3}.\n\nIf k=3: allowed (k,l):\n- (3,2): l=2\n- (3,0): l=0\n\nThus l ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow c12 (l,i): allowed pairs for (l,i):\n- (3,2): l=3,i=2\n- (0,1): l=0,i=1\n- (2,0): l=2,i=0\n- (1,0): l=1,i=0\n- (1,3): l=1,i=3\n- (3,0): l=3,i=0\n\nThus for each possible (l) we have possible i values.\n\nNow c9 (i,j): j=3. Allowed pairs for (i,j):\n- (2,3): i=2\n- (0,0): i=0, j=0 not match j=3.\n- (3,0): j=0 not match.\n- (0,2): j=2 not match.\n- (2,1): j=1 not match.\n- (0,3): i=0, j=3 allowed.\n\nThus i can be either 2 (via (2,3)) or 0 (via (0,3)). So i ∈ {0,2}.\n\nThus i ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow we need to satisfy c12 with l and i, and also c18 linking f and i.\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: (0,2) or (3,3). Since i ∈ {0,2}, only (0,2) possible: f=0,i=2. So i must be 2, and f must be 0.\n\nThus i=2, f=0.\n\nThus i=2 is forced. Then f=0.\n\nThus we have f=0.\n\nNow check earlier possibilities for (e,f) combos: we need to see if (e,f) = (e,0) is allowed.\n\nRecall possible (e,f) combos: (0,1), (1,0), (1,3). So (e,f) = (1,0) is allowed. So e must be 1. So e=1.\n\nThus e=1, f=0.\n\nNow we need to check earlier branch for e,f,g,h. Let's see if e=1,f=0 leads to consistent g,h.\n\nFrom earlier analysis:\n\nCase (e=1,f=0):\n- f=0 => c6: g=3.\n- g=3 => h=0.\n- h=0 => c8: e can be 0 or 1. e=1 allowed. So consistent.\n\nThus g=3, h=0.\n\nThus we have g=3, h=0.\n\nNow check c7 (g,h): (3,0) allowed. Yes.\n\nc8 (h,e): (0,1) allowed. Yes.\n\nThus branch (e=1,f=0,g=3,h=0) works.\n\nThus we have assignments:\n\na=3\nb=0\nc=3\nd=1\ne=1\nf=0\ng=3\nh=0\ni=2\nj=3\nk ∈ {1,3}\nl depends on k\nm=1\nn=0\no=0\np=1\n\nNow we need to assign k and l consistent with constraints.\n\nWe have k ∈ {1,3}. Let's explore both.\n\nCase 1: k=1.\n- Then from c11, l ∈ {1,3} (since k=1).\n- So l ∈ {1,3}.\n\nNow c12 (l,i) with i=2:\n- If l=1: allowed pairs for (l,i) = (1,0) or (1,3). Neither has i=2. So l=1 invalid.\n- If l=3: allowed pairs for (l,i) = (3,2) or (3,0). (3,2) matches i=2. So l=3 works (via (3,2)). Also (3,0) would be i=0 not match. So l=3 is allowed.\n\nThus k=1,l=3 works.\n\nThus we have k=1,l=3.\n\nNow check c11 (k,l) with k=1,l=3: allowed (1,3) yes.\n\nThus this assignment works.\n\nCase 2: k=3.\n- Then from c11, l ∈ {0,2}.\n- So l ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow c12 (l,i) with i=2:\n- If l=0: allowed pairs for (l,i) = (0,1) or (0,0) or (0,2)? Actually (0,1) i=1 not match; (0,0) i=0 not match; (0,2) is not listed. Wait c12 allowed pairs are:\n  - (3,2): l=3,i=2\n  - (0,1): l=0,i=1\n  - (2,0): l=2,i=0\n  - (1,0): l=1,i=0\n  - (1,3): l=1,i=3\n  - (3,0): l=3,i=0\n\nThus l=0 only allows i=1 (via (0,1)). So l=0 invalid for i=2.\n\n- If l=2: allowed (2,0): i=0 not match. So l=2 invalid.\n\nThus k=3 leads to no valid l for i=2. So k cannot be 3.\n\nThus we must have k=1, l=3.\n\nThus we have a full assignment:\n\na=3\nb=0\nc=3\nd=1\ne=1\nf=0\ng=3\nh=0\ni=2\nj=3\nk=1\nl=3\nm=1\nn=0\no=0\np=1\n\nNow verify all constraints to be sure.\n\nLet's list all constraints and check each:\n\nc1 (a,b) = (3,0): allowed? Yes (3,0) is in allowed pairs list for c1.\n\nc2 (b,c) = (0,3): allowed? Yes (0,3) in list.\n\nc3 (c,d) = (3,1): allowed? Yes (3,1) in list.\n\nc4 (d,a) = (1,3): allowed? Yes (1,3) in list.\n\nc5 (e,f) = (1,0): allowed? Yes (1,0) in list.\n\nc6 (f,g) = (0,3): allowed? Yes (0,3) in list.\n\nc7 (g,h) = (3,0): allowed? Yes (3,0) in list.\n\nc8 (h,e) = (0,1): allowed? Yes (0,1) in list.\n\nc9 (i,j) = (2,3): allowed? Yes (2,3) in list.\n\nc10 (j,k) = (3,1): allowed? Yes (3,1) in list.\n\nc11 (k,l) = (1,3): allowed? Yes (1,3) in list.\n\nc12 (l,i) = (3,2): allowed? Yes (3,2) in list.\n\nc13 (m,n) = (1,0): allowed? Yes (1,0) in list.\n\nc14 (n,o) = (0,0): allowed? Yes (0,0) in list.\n\nc15 (o,p) = (0,1): allowed? Yes (0,1) in list.\n\nc16 (p,m) = (1,1): allowed? Yes (1,1) in list.\n\nc17 (b,e) = (0,1): allowed? Yes (0,1) in list.\n\nc18 (f,i) = (0,2): allowed? Yes (0,2) in list.\n\nc19 (j,m) = (3,1): allowed? Yes (3,1) in list.\n\nc20 (n,a) = (0,3): allowed? Yes (0,3) in list.\n\nAll constraints satisfied.\n\nThus we have a valid assignment.\n\nNow the answer requirements: \"Give one complete assignment for a through p satisfying all twenty constraints.\" So we need to output JSON envelope with assignment values.\n\nThe envelope template is given. We need to fill assignment fields with the values we found.\n\nThus the JSON:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6112-raw-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 3,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 3,\n        \"d\": 1,\n        \"e\": 1,\n        \"f\": 0,\n        \"g\": 3,\n        \"h\": 0,\n        \"i\": 2,\n        \"j\": 3,\n        \"k\": 1,\n        \"l\": 3,\n        \"m\": 1,\n        \"n\": 0,\n        \"o\": 0,\n        \"p\": 1\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6112\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6112\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\"\n}\n\nWe need to output raw JSON without markdown code fences. Ensure proper formatting. Also note that the envelope template includes \"conclusion\": { \"assignment\": {...} }. The values must be JSON integers.\n\nThus final answer is that JSON.\n\nWe should double-check that the envelope's structure matches exactly the template. The template includes \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\", \"message_id\": \"csp-6112-raw-integrator-1\", \"payload\": { \"conclusion\": { \"assignment\": {...} } }, \"problem_id\": \"csp-6112\", \"read_refs\": [ { \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6112\", \"revision\": 1, \"state_revision\": 1 } ], \"recipients\": [ \"shared-integrator\" ], \"reply_to\": null, \"schema_version\": 1, \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\". Yes.\n\nThus output exactly that JSON.\n\nNow we should double-check that there is no extra whitespace or newline? It's fine.\n\nNow check the answer requirement: \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" Not sure what that means. Possibly they want to list all possible assignments for each component? However the envelope only includes assignment. The problem statement says \"Give one complete assignment for a through p satisfying all twenty constraints.\" So we are fine.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope.\n\nWe need to ensure we output exactly the JSON with no extra commentary. So produce the JSON.\n\nThus final.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve a CSP: variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p each domain {0,1,2,3}. Constraints c1-c16 are binary constraints on pairs, each specifying allowed pairs (ordered). Also there are \"bridge\" constraints c17-c20 linking variables across components: c17: allowed pairs for (b,e) are only [0,1] and [0,0] (so b=0, e=0 or b=0, e=1). c18: (f,i) allowed pairs: [0,2] and [3,3]. c19: (j,m) allowed: [3,1] and [3,2]. c20: (n,a) allowed: [0,3] and [3,0].\n\nWe need to find a complete assignment that satisfies all constraints. Also local requirement: \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" Not entirely clear: The answer_requirements say: \"Give one complete assignment for a through p satisfying all twenty constraints.\" So we need to produce a single assignment. \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" That seems contradictory; maybe we need to output all satisfying tuples for each component? But the envelope expects assignment only. Might be extraneous description. The main requirement: Return a complete assignment for all 16 variables satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe need to solve the CSP. Let's attempt systematically.\n\nWe have constraints c1-c16: each constraint is a binary allowed pairs list for a specific ordered pair of variables. We need to assign values to a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p ∈ {0,1,2,3} such that each pair (var1,var2) belongs to allowed_pairs for that constraint.\n\nThe constraints form a cycle: a-b (c1), b-c (c2), c-d (c3), d-a (c4), e-f (c5), f-g (c6), g-h (c7), h-e (c8), i-j (c9), j-k (c10), k-l (c11), l-i (c12), m-n (c13), n-o (c14), o-p (c15), p-m (c16). Bridges: b-e (c17), f-i (c18), j-m (c19), n-a (c20). So we have two disconnected components? Actually there are three groups: group1 includes a,b,c,d and also e,f,g,h via bridges, plus i,j,k,l via bridges? Let's see connections: Bridges connect across groups: b-e links group1 (a,b,c,d) to group2 (e,f,g,h). f-i links group2 to group3 (i,j,k,l). j-m links group3 to group4 (m,n,o,p). n-a links group4 back to group1. So overall it's a single connected graph.\n\nWe need to satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe can brute force search manually or via reasoning.\n\nWe have small domain size 4, 16 variables, total combos 4^16 huge. But constraints heavily restrict.\n\nLet's enumerate constraints.\n\nc1 (a,b): allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (2,2), (2,3), (0,3), (1,3). So allowed (a,b):\n- a=3,b=0\n- a=0,b=2\n- a=2,b=2\n- a=2,b=3\n- a=0,b=3\n- a=1,b=3\n\nc2 (b,c): allowed: (0,3), (2,3), (1,2), (3,2), (2,1), (3,0). So (b,c):\n- b=0,c=3\n- b=2,c=3\n- b=1,c=2\n- b=3,c=2\n- b=2,c=1\n- b=3,c=0\n\nc3 (c,d): allowed: (3,1), (0,3), (3,3), (3,0), (0,2), (2,2). So (c,d):\n- c=3,d=1\n- c=0,d=3\n- c=3,d=3\n- c=3,d=0\n- c=0,d=2\n- c=2,d=2\n\nc4 (d,a): allowed: (1,3), (1,0), (1,1), (0,1), (3,2), (2,1). So (d,a):\n- d=1,a=3\n- d=1,a=0\n- d=1,a=1\n- d=0,a=1\n- d=3,a=2\n- d=2,a=1\n\nc5 (e,f): allowed: (1,0), (1,3), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2), (0,1). So (e,f):\n- e=1,f=0\n- e=1,f=3\n- e=2,f=2\n- e=2,f=0\n- e=3,f=2\n- e=0,f=1\n\nc6 (f,g): allowed: (0,3), (2,0), (3,3), (2,2), (1,3), (3,1). So (f,g):\n- f=0,g=3\n- f=2,g=0\n- f=3,g=3\n- f=2,g=2\n- f=1,g=3\n- f=3,g=1\n\nc7 (g,h): allowed: (3,0), (1,0), (2,1), (2,3), (1,3), (2,0). So (g,h):\n- g=3,h=0\n- g=1,h=0\n- g=2,h=1\n- g=2,h=3\n- g=1,h=3\n- g=2,h=0\n\nc8 (h,e): allowed: (0,1), (0,0), (1,2), (3,3), (3,1), (1,0). So (h,e):\n- h=0,e=1\n- h=0,e=0\n- h=1,e=2\n- h=3,e=3\n- h=3,e=1\n- h=1,e=0\n\nc9 (i,j): allowed: (2,3), (0,0), (3,0), (0,2), (2,1), (0,3). So (i,j):\n- i=2,j=3\n- i=0,j=0\n- i=3,j=0\n- i=0,j=2\n- i=2,j=1\n- i=0,j=3\n\nc10 (j,k): allowed: (3,1), (3,3), (1,3), (2,3), (1,2), (2,1). So (j,k):\n- j=3,k=1\n- j=3,k=3\n- j=1,k=3\n- j=2,k=3\n- j=1,k=2\n- j=2,k=1\n\nc11 (k,l): allowed: (1,3), (1,1), (3,2), (0,3), (3,0), (0,2). So (k,l):\n- k=1,l=3\n- k=1,l=1\n- k=3,l=2\n- k=0,l=3\n- k=3,l=0\n- k=0,l=2\n\nc12 (l,i): allowed: (3,2), (0,1), (2,0), (1,0), (1,3), (3,0). So (l,i):\n- l=3,i=2\n- l=0,i=1\n- l=2,i=0\n- l=1,i=0\n- l=1,i=3\n- l=3,i=0\n\nc13 (m,n): allowed: (1,0), (2,3), (1,3), (0,2), (1,1), (3,0). So (m,n):\n- m=1,n=0\n- m=2,n=3\n- m=1,n=3\n- m=0,n=2\n- m=1,n=1\n- m=3,n=0\n\nc14 (n,o): allowed: (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,3). So (n,o):\n- n=0,o=0\n- n=1,o=3\n- n=0,o=2\n- n=2,o=1\n- n=2,o=3\n- n=3,o=3\n\nc15 (o,p): allowed: (0,1), (0,0), (1,3), (1,0), (2,3), (3,2). So (o,p):\n- o=0,p=1\n- o=0,p=0\n- o=1,p=3\n- o=1,p=0\n- o=2,p=3\n- o=3,p=2\n\nc16 (p,m): allowed: (1,1), (3,3), (0,2), (1,0), (2,3), (3,0). So (p,m):\n- p=1,m=1\n- p=3,m=3\n- p=0,m=2\n- p=1,m=0\n- p=2,m=3\n- p=3,m=0\n\nc17 (b,e): allowed: (0,1), (0,0). So b=0,e=0 or b=0,e=1.\n\nc18 (f,i): allowed: (0,2), (3,3). So f=0,i=2 OR f=3,i=3.\n\nc19 (j,m): allowed: (3,1), (3,2). So j=3,m=1 OR j=3,m=2.\n\nc20 (n,a): allowed: (0,3), (3,0). So n=0,a=3 OR n=3,a=0.\n\nWe need to assign all variables accordingly.\n\nLet's approach systematically.\n\nWe have many constraints. Let's try to reduce possibilities.\n\nFirst, c17 forces b=0. So b=0. Then c17's allowed pairs: (b,e) = (0,1) or (0,0). So e ∈ {0,1}.\n\nThus b=0.\n\nNow c1 (a,b): b=0. Allowed pairs for (a,b):\n- (3,0) => a=3,b=0 allowed.\n- (0,2) => a=0,b=2 not allowed because b=0.\n- (2,2) => b=2 not allowed.\n- (2,3) => b=3 not allowed.\n- (0,3) => b=3 not allowed.\n- (1,3) => b=3 not allowed.\n\nThus only allowed pair with b=0 is (3,0). So a=3.\n\nThus a=3.\n\nNow c4 (d,a): a=3. Allowed pairs for (d,a):\n- (1,3): d=1,a=3 allowed.\n- (1,0): a=0 not allowed.\n- (1,1): a=1 not allowed.\n- (0,1): a=1 not allowed.\n- (3,2): a=2 not allowed.\n- (2,1): a=1 not allowed.\n\nThus only allowed is (d=1,a=3). So d=1.\n\nThus d=1.\n\nNow c3 (c,d): d=1. Allowed pairs for (c,d):\n- (3,1): c=3,d=1 allowed.\n- (0,3): d=3 not allowed.\n- (3,3): d=3 not allowed.\n- (3,0): d=0 not allowed.\n- (0,2): d=2 not allowed.\n- (2,2): d=2 not allowed.\n\nThus only allowed is (c=3,d=1). So c=3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c2 (b,c): b=0,c=3. Check allowed pairs for (b,c):\n- (0,3): b=0,c=3 allowed.\n- (2,3): b=2 not allowed.\n- (1,2): b=1 not allowed.\n- (3,2): b=3 not allowed.\n- (2,1): b=2 not allowed.\n- (3,0): b=3 not allowed.\n\nThus only allowed is (0,3). So this matches. Good.\n\nThus a,b,c,d solved: a=3,b=0,c=3,d=1.\n\nNow we have e from c17: b=0 => e ∈ {0,1}. So e=0 or 1.\n\nNow c5 (e,f): depends on e. Let's list possibilities for e.\n\nCase 1: e=0. Then c5 allowed pairs (e,f):\n- (1,0): e=1 not allowed.\n- (1,3): e=1 not allowed.\n- (2,2): e=2 not allowed.\n- (2,0): e=2 not allowed.\n- (3,2): e=3 not allowed.\n- (0,1): e=0,f=1 allowed.\n\nThus if e=0, then f must be 1.\n\nCase 2: e=1. Then c5 allowed pairs:\n- (1,0): e=1,f=0 allowed.\n- (1,3): e=1,f=3 allowed.\n- (2,2): e=2 not allowed.\n- (2,0): e=2 not allowed.\n- (3,2): e=3 not allowed.\n- (0,1): e=0 not allowed.\n\nThus if e=1, f ∈ {0,3}.\n\nThus possible (e,f) combos:\n- (0,1)\n- (1,0)\n- (1,3)\n\nNow c6 (f,g): depends on f. Let's consider each f.\n\nIf f=1: c6 allowed (f,g):\n- (0,3): f=0 not allowed.\n- (2,0): f=2 not allowed.\n- (3,3): f=3 not allowed.\n- (2,2): f=2 not allowed.\n- (1,3): f=1,g=3 allowed.\n- (3,1): f=3 not allowed.\n\nThus if f=1, then g=3.\n\nIf f=0: c6 allowed:\n- (0,3): f=0,g=3 allowed.\n- (2,0): f=2 not allowed.\n- (3,3): f=3 not allowed.\n- (2,2): f=2 not allowed.\n- (1,3): f=1 not allowed.\n- (3,1): f=3 not allowed.\n\nThus if f=0, then g=3.\n\nIf f=3: c6 allowed:\n- (0,3): f=0 not allowed.\n- (2,0): f=2 not allowed.\n- (3,3): f=3,g=3 allowed.\n- (2,2): f=2 not allowed.\n- (1,3): f=1 not allowed.\n- (3,1): f=3,g=1 allowed.\n\nThus if f=3, then g ∈ {3,1}.\n\nThus g possibilities:\n- If f=1 => g=3.\n- If f=0 => g=3.\n- If f=3 => g=3 or g=1.\n\nNow c7 (g,h): allowed pairs for (g,h):\n- (3,0): g=3,h=0 allowed.\n- (1,0): g=1,h=0 allowed.\n- (2,1): g=2,h=1 not applicable (g not 2).\n- (2,3): g=2,h=3 not applicable.\n- (1,3): g=1,h=3 allowed.\n- (2,0): g=2,h=0 not applicable.\n\nThus g can be 3 or 1. If g=3, h can be 0 (via (3,0)) or maybe also? Check (g=3) other allowed pairs: (3,0) only? There's also (3,?) Not other. So g=3 => h=0.\n\nIf g=1, then h can be 0 (via (1,0)) or 3 (via (1,3)). So h ∈ {0,3} if g=1.\n\nNow c8 (h,e): allowed pairs for (h,e):\n- (0,1): h=0,e=1 allowed.\n- (0,0): h=0,e=0 allowed.\n- (1,2): h=1,e=2 not applicable (h not 1).\n- (3,3): h=3,e=3 not applicable (e not 3 maybe).\n- (3,1): h=3,e=1 allowed.\n- (1,0): h=1,e=0 not applicable.\n\nThus possibilities:\n- If h=0: e can be 1 (via (0,1)) or 0 (via (0,0)). So e ∈ {0,1} when h=0.\n- If h=3: e can be 1 (via (3,1)) or 3 (via (3,3)). So e ∈ {1,3} when h=3.\n- If h=1: not allowed (no pair with h=1? Actually (1,2) and (1,0) require e=2 or e=0; but (1,2) is allowed but e=2 not in domain? e domain is 0-3, e=2 possible. However (1,2) is allowed: h=1,e=2. Also (1,0) allowed: h=1,e=0. So h=1 also possible: e ∈ {2,0}. But is h=1 reachable? g=2? But g cannot be 2 based on earlier constraints? Let's check: c7 allowed g values: 3,1,2? Actually c7 allowed pairs include (2,1) and (2,3) and (2,0) but those require g=2. But we have g from earlier: g can be 3 or 1 (if f=3 maybe also 1). Actually from c6, g can be 3 or 1 (if f=3). So g cannot be 2. So h cannot be 1 because g cannot be 2. So h ∈ {0,3}.\n\nThus h must be 0 or 3.\n\nNow combine with earlier constraints: h is determined by g.\n\nCase A: g=3 => h=0 (only). Then h=0. Then c8: h=0 => e ∈ {0,1}.\n\nCase B: g=1 => h ∈ {0,3}. So possibilities: (g=1,h=0) or (g=1,h=3).\n\nNow let's examine the possibilities for e and f and g consistent with c5-c8.\n\nWe have initial possibilities for (e,f):\n- (0,1)\n- (1,0)\n- (1,3)\n\nNow evaluate each:\n\nCase (e=0,f=1):\n- f=1 => from c6, g=3.\n- Then g=3 => h=0.\n- Then h=0 => c8 allows e=0 or 1. e=0 matches, ok.\nThus (e=0,f=1,g=3,h=0) is consistent.\n\nCheck c8: (h=0,e=0) is allowed (0,0). Good.\n\nThus one consistent branch.\n\nCase (e=1,f=0):\n- f=0 => c6: g=3.\n- g=3 => h=0.\n- h=0 => c8 allows e=0 or 1. e=1 allowed (0,1) pair.\nThus (e=1,f=0,g=3,h=0) consistent.\n\nCase (e=1,f=3):\n- f=3 => c6: g ∈ {3,1}.\nWe need to consider subcases:\n\nSubcase (e=1,f=3,g=3):\n- g=3 => h=0.\n- h=0 => c8 allows e=0 or 1. e=1 allowed.\nThus (e=1,f=3,g=3,h=0) consistent.\n\nSubcase (e=1,f=3,g=1):\n- g=1 => h ∈ {0,3}.\nNow need to satisfy c8 with h and e=1.\n\nIf h=0: c8 allows e=0 or 1 => e=1 ok.\nIf h=3: c8 allows e=1 or 3 => e=1 ok.\nThus both h=0 and h=3 possible.\n\nThus (e=1,f=3,g=1,h=0) and (e=1,f=3,g=1,h=3) are possible.\n\nThus many possibilities for e,f,g,h.\n\nNow we also have constraints linking to other variables via bridges: c17 already used (b,e) satisfied: b=0, e ∈ {0,1} consistent. c18 (f,i): allowed pairs: (0,2) or (3,3). So depending on f, i is determined.\n\nc19 (j,m): j=3,m ∈ {1,2}.\n\nc20 (n,a): a=3 => n must be? Allowed pairs: (0,3) => n=0,a=3 allowed; (3,0) => n=3,a=0 not allowed because a=3. So only n=0 works. So n=0.\n\nThus n=0.\n\nNow c13 (m,n): n=0. Allowed pairs for (m,n):\n- (1,0): m=1,n=0 allowed.\n- (2,3): n=3 not allowed.\n- (1,3): n=3 not allowed.\n- (0,2): n=2 not allowed.\n- (1,1): n=1 not allowed.\n- (3,0): m=3,n=0 allowed.\n\nThus m ∈ {1,3}.\n\nNow c14 (n,o): n=0. Allowed pairs for (n,o):\n- (0,0): o=0 allowed.\n- (1,3): n=1 not allowed.\n- (0,2): o=2 allowed.\n- (2,1): n=2 not allowed.\n- (2,3): n=2 not allowed.\n- (3,3): n=3 not allowed.\n\nThus o ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow c15 (o,p): depends on o.\n\nIf o=0: allowed pairs:\n- (0,1): p=1 allowed.\n- (0,0): p=0 allowed.\n- (1,3): o=1 not allowed.\n- (1,0): o=1 not allowed.\n- (2,3): o=2 not allowed.\n- (3,2): o=3 not allowed.\n\nThus if o=0, p ∈ {0,1}.\n\nIf o=2: allowed pairs:\n- (0,1): o=0 not allowed.\n- (0,0): o=0 not allowed.\n- (1,3): o=1 not allowed.\n- (1,0): o=1 not allowed.\n- (2,3): p=3 allowed.\n- (3,2): o=3 not allowed.\n\nThus if o=2, p=3.\n\nThus p possibilities:\n- If o=0 => p ∈ {0,1}\n- If o=2 => p=3\n\nNow c16 (p,m): allowed pairs for (p,m):\n- (1,1): p=1,m=1 allowed.\n- (3,3): p=3,m=3 allowed.\n- (0,2): p=0,m=2 allowed.\n- (1,0): p=1,m=0 allowed.\n- (2,3): p=2,m=3 allowed.\n- (3,0): p=3,m=0 allowed.\n\nThus given p and m must satisfy one of these.\n\nWe have m ∈ {1,3} from earlier. So possible combos:\n\nIf m=1:\n- Allowed pairs with m=1:\n  - (1,1): p=1\n  - (1,0): p=1,m=0 not relevant.\n  - (3,3): p=3,m=3 not relevant.\n  - (0,2): m=2 not relevant.\n  - (2,3): m=3 not relevant.\n  - (3,0): m=0 not relevant.\n\nThus only p=1 works for m=1. So if m=1, then p must be 1.\n\nIf m=3:\n- Allowed pairs with m=3:\n  - (3,3): p=3\n  - (2,3): p=2\n  - (3,0): p=3,m=0 not relevant.\n  - (1,1): m=1 not relevant.\n  - (1,0): m=0 not relevant.\n  - (0,2): m=2 not relevant.\n\nThus p ∈ {2,3} for m=3.\n\nNow combine with earlier constraints on p from o.\n\nIf o=0 => p ∈ {0,1}. Intersection with p possibilities:\n\n- If m=1 => p must be 1. That fits o=0 (p=1 allowed). So (o=0,p=1,m=1) works.\n- If m=3 => p ∈ {2,3}. Intersection with {0,1} is empty. So if o=0, m cannot be 3. So m must be 1.\n\nThus we deduce: o=0, m=1, p=1.\n\nNow check if o=2 possible? If o=2 => p=3. Then p=3 must satisfy c16 with m.\n\n- If m=1 => p=3 not allowed (p must be 1). So m cannot be 1.\n- If m=3 => p=3 allowed (c16 (3,3)). So m=3, p=3, o=2 works.\n\nThus second possible branch: o=2, p=3, m=3.\n\nThus we have two possible branches for (m,n,o,p):\nBranch A: n=0, m=1, o=0, p=1.\nBranch B: n=0, m=3, o=2, p=3.\n\nNow we need to satisfy other constraints: c13 (m,n) satisfied by both branches (m=1,n=0) is allowed (1,0). (m=3,n=0) also allowed (3,0). Good.\n\nc14 (n,o) satisfied: n=0,o=0 allowed (0,0). n=0,o=2 allowed (0,2). Good.\n\nc15 (o,p) satisfied: o=0,p=1 allowed (0,1). o=2,p=3 allowed (2,3). Good.\n\nc16 (p,m) satisfied: p=1,m=1 allowed (1,1). p=3,m=3 allowed (3,3). Good.\n\nThus both branches are viable.\n\nNow we need to handle other variables: i,j,k,l from the other component.\n\nWe have constraints:\n\nc9 (i,j): allowed pairs:\n- (2,3): i=2,j=3\n- (0,0): i=0,j=0\n- (3,0): i=3,j=0\n- (0,2): i=0,j=2\n- (2,1): i=2,j=1\n- (0,3): i=0,j=3\n\nThus i can be 0,2,3. j accordingly.\n\nc10 (j,k): allowed pairs:\n- (3,1): j=3,k=1\n- (3,3): j=3,k=3\n- (1,3): j=1,k=3\n- (2,3): j=2,k=3\n- (1,2): j=1,k=2\n- (2,1): j=2,k=1\n\nThus j can be 1,2,3. k accordingly.\n\nc11 (k,l): allowed pairs:\n- (1,3): k=1,l=3\n- (1,1): k=1,l=1\n- (3,2): k=3,l=2\n- (0,3): k=0,l=3\n- (3,0): k=3,l=0\n- (0,2): k=0,l=2\n\nThus k can be 0,1,3. l accordingly.\n\nc12 (l,i): allowed pairs:\n- (3,2): l=3,i=2\n- (0,1): l=0,i=1\n- (2,0): l=2,i=0\n- (1,0): l=1,i=0\n- (1,3): l=1,i=3\n- (3,0): l=3,i=0\n\nThus l can be 0,1,2,3. i accordingly.\n\nc18 (f,i): allowed pairs: (0,2) or (3,3). So depending on f.\n\nc19 (j,m): allowed pairs: (3,1) or (3,2). So j must be 3, and m is 1 or 2 accordingly. Since we have m already determined in branch A (m=1) or branc